One thing we've learned is how flexible or plastic the brain is, because especially when we're trying to, we're using these non-biomimetic decoders. Remember, if I talked about that, that is you take signals from the brain, send them through an arbitrary decoder that it's not something that's natural for the subject. The subject has to learn how to activate the right pattern of activity of cells in order to move the device the right way. And what's remarkable is that both animals and humans can do that. And it doesn't take that much time. But, I mean, it takes some time, but it's like learning a motor skill, like learning how to ride your bike or play a musical instrument.
我们学到的一件事是大脑有多灵活或可塑，因为特别是当我们尝试时，我们正在使用这些非仿生解码器。记得我谈到过那个吗？那就是你从大脑获取信号，通过一个任意解码器发送它们，这不是受试者自然的东西。受试者必须学习如何激活正确的细胞活动模式，以便以正确的方式移动设备。令人惊奇的是，动物和人类都能做到这一点。而且不需要太多时间。但是，我的意思是，它需要一些时间，但这就像学习运动技能，比如学习如何骑自行车或演奏乐器。

25
00:16:50.000 --> 00:17:34.000
It does take time, but you can learn how to do this. So you're taking now a part of the brain that had never been exposed to controlling this device in a completely arbitrary way. And it figures out how to do it. I think that that plasticity, that flexibility is remarkable. And by the way, another thing that other people are looking at as well is, I mean, you can, you don't have to focus just on the motor areas of the brain. You can look at other parts of the brain, and people have shown that even in other parts of the brain that you normally wouldn't think are associated with moving, you can use those, again,
确实需要时间，但你可以学会如何做到这一点。所以你现在正在利用大脑中从未接触过以完全任意方式控制该设备的部分。它弄清楚了如何做到这一点。我认为那种可塑性，那种灵活性是了不起的。顺便说一句，其他人也在研究的另一件事是，我的意思是，你可以，你不必只关注大脑的运动区域。你可以观察大脑的其他部分，人们已经证明，即使在大脑的其他部分，你通常不会认为与移动相关，你也可以使用它们，再次，

26
00:17:34.000 --> 00:18:29.000
with this sort of non-biomatic approach where the subject or the animal has to learn and the animal learns and figures out how to do it. And so it just, it shows you the flexibility of the brain, which is remarkable. There are people out there, probably most famously Elon Musk, who are talking about a future where brain machine interfaces are used for augmenting people's abilities, not just for helping people who have disabilities. Do you see that happening? I mean, will we all someday be able to access the Internet directly with our brains? And is that something that you think we'll want to do? I'm not sure we want to do that, but it will probably happen if we solve a problem that still faces us in the field, which is a way to non-invasively implant or
用这种非仿生方法，受试者或动物必须学习，动物学习并弄清楚如何做到这一点。所以它只是，它向你展示了大脑的灵活性，这是了不起的。外面有人，可能最著名的是埃隆·马斯克，他们正在谈论一个脑机接口用于增强人们能力的未来，而不仅仅是帮助残疾人。你看到这种情况发生了吗？我的意思是，我们所有人终有一天都能用大脑直接访问互联网吗？这是你认为我们会想做的事情吗？我不确定我们是否想那样做，但如果我们解决了该领域仍然面临的问题，即一种非侵入性植入或

27
00:18:29.000 --> 00:19:34.000
record signals from the brain. At the resolution, we need that. We need to. So I mean, right now you can use EEG electrodes that are sitting on the scalp and created a brain computer interface. They work not very well. So if you really want an effective brain computer interface, you need to access signals at the level of single neurons or small groups of neurons. And there isn't really a device out there that's non-invasive at the present moment. And so if it's not invasive, you know, how many people are really going to volunteer to have brain surgery, open up their skull? I mean, now there are technologies out there that are being, there are several companies out there right now, as we speak, that are trying to create minimally invasive technologies. So one approach, for example, is you only have to create a small little cut in the skull, very, very small, and slip in a set of electrodes.
记录大脑信号的方法，它可能会发生。在我们需要的分辨率下。我们需要。所以我的意思是，现在你可以使用坐在头皮上的脑电图电极并创建一个脑机接口。它们工作得不是很好。所以如果你真的想要一个有效的脑机接口，你需要获取单个神经元或一小群神经元水平的信号。目前还没有真正非侵入性的设备。所以如果它不是侵入性的，你知道，有多少人真的会自愿进行脑部手术，打开他们的头骨？我的意思是，现在有技术正在被，现在有几家公司，就在我们说话的时候，正在尝试创造微创技术。所以一种方法，例如，你只需要在头骨上做一个小切口，非常非常小，然后滑入一组电极。

28
00:19:34.000 --> 00:20:12.000
And these electrodes are sitting on the brain, not in the brain. Very high density electrodes. It's minimal, but it's still surgery. Another approach is by a company called Synchron out of Australia. They're trying to implant electrodes through the. blood vessels, which they then feed up. So they stick that up. I'm not sure exactly where. And somewhere in the body, they stick the electrodes inside the blood vessels, and they feed it up to the brain and then record signals from the brain through the blood vessels, through the
这些电极坐在大脑上，而不是在大脑里。高密度电极。它是微创的，但仍然是手术。另一种方法是由澳大利亚的一家名为Synchron的公司提出的。他们正试图通过血管植入电极，然后他们将其送入。所以他们把它插进去。我不确定具体在哪里。在身体的某个地方，他们把电极插进血管里，然后把它送到大脑，然后通过血管，通过血管壁记录大脑的信号。

29
00:20:12.000 --> 00:21:06.000
walls of the blood vessels. I'm not sure if that's actually going to work because you still have, you're not getting really, really close to the neurons. But those. are two approaches that are minimally invasive, that might result in larger adoption of this technology, but I don't know. We don't have the technology. It just doesn't exist to actually non-invasively record from the brain at the resolution we need. What are you working on now? And what are your biggest research priorities? Our research priorities right now is to augment these brain machine interfaces with sensory feedback. So with movement, and this is something I ignored for years, but it's very important. So when we normally move, we rely besides vision, which is important,
我不确定这是否真的有效，因为你仍然有，你并没有真正非常接近神经元。但那些是两种微创方法，可能会导致这种技术的更大采用，但我不知道。我们没有这项技术。它根本不存在，无法以我们需要的分辨率非侵入性地记录大脑。你现在在做什么？你最大的研究重点是什么？我们现在的研究重点是利用感觉反馈来增强这些脑机接口。所以对于运动，这是我多年来忽略的事情，但它非常重要。所以当我们正常移动时，除了视觉（这很重要）之外，我们依赖，

30
00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:59.000
we have to be able to see what we're doing. But we can do a lot of things without vision. And what's really important is touch, especially when you're interacting with objects. You want to touch an object like a cup. You want to make sure you grab it with enough force so it doesn't slip, but not too much that you break it. So the touch is important. And another sense called proprioception or kinesthesia, the sense of feeling your limbs in space and how they're moving and where they are in space. Those two senses are really important for normal movement. And I know, we know this from patients that lack that sense, those two senses. If you go on YouTube, you can see videos, and they're severely motor disabled. Even though their motor system is completely intact,
我们必须能够看到我们在做什么。但我们可以在没有视觉的情况下做很多事情。真正重要的是触觉，尤其是在你与物体交互时。你想触摸一个像杯子一样的物体。你想确保你用足够的力抓住它，这样它就不会滑落，但不要太用力以至于弄碎它。所以触觉很重要。另一种感觉称为本体感觉或动觉，即在空间中感受你的肢体以及它们如何移动以及它们在空间中的位置的感觉。这两种感觉对于正常运动非常重要。我知道，我们从缺乏那种感觉的患者那里知道这一点，那两种感觉。如果你去YouTube，你可以看到视频，他们严重运动残疾。即使他们的运动系统完全完好，

31
00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:56.000
they look like they've had a motor injury, but they don't have those senses. So that's what we're trying to do right now. And currently, we're augmenting these systems with a sense of touch. So what we do is we have now two human subjects at the University of Chicago, and there are others as well at the University of Pittsburgh with whom we work. And these subjects have electrodes both in the motor areas, motor cortex, for control, but electrodes also in the somatosensory cortex, which is right next to the motor cortex. and we stimulate electrically. Instead of record, we send signals into the electrodes, electrical signals, to stimulate those areas near the electrodes. And these subjects report feeling tactile touch sensations on the fingertips.
他们看起来像受过运动损伤，但他们没有那些感觉。所以这就是我们现在正在尝试做的事情。目前，我们正在用触觉增强这些系统。所以我们现在在芝加哥大学有两名人类受试者，在匹兹堡大学也有其他人与我们合作。这些受试者在运动区域（运动皮层）都有电极用于控制，但在体感皮层（就在运动皮层旁边）也有电极。我们进行电刺激。我们不是记录，我们发送信号到电极，电信号，来刺激电极附近的那些区域。这些受试者报告说在指尖上有触觉感。

32
00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:31.000
So now we now have what's called a bidirectional brain computer interface. We're going both out of the brain and into the brain in both directions. And so with that, you can now imagine a scenario where a subject is controlling a robotic hand. They can reach out, grab an object like a cup. They then feel a cup and manipulate it in a way that they don't do damage and they don't break the cup. And they feel it. They actually feel what it feels like on their fingertips. So that's what we're developing now.
所以我们现在有了所谓的双向脑机接口。我们正在双向进出大脑。因此，你可以想象一个场景，受试者正在控制一只机械手。他们可以伸出手，抓住一个像杯子一样的物体。然后他们感觉到杯子并以一种不会造成损坏且不会弄碎杯子的方式操纵它。他们感觉到了它。他们实际上感觉到了指尖上的感觉。所以这就是我们现在正在开发的。

33
00:23:31.000 --> 00:24:05.000
So we've mentioned the sense of touch. We've talked about a little bit, the hearing because cochlear implants have been around for a while now. You talked about swallowing, but there are several other senses. I'm just wondering, is anybody looking into prosthetic eyes? Will we ever be able to give people back vision? Yes. So there are several groups looking at artificial vision. And, yeah, that's, it's not, it hasn't resulted in a commercial product as far as I know.
所以我们提到了触觉。我们谈到了一点，听觉，因为人工耳蜗已经存在一段时间了。你谈到了吞咽，但还有其他几种感觉。我只是在想，有没有人在研究假眼？我们将来能让人恢复视力吗？是的。所以有几个小组在研究人工视觉。而且，是的，据我所知，它还没有产生商业产品。

34
00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:45.000
Although it's close. But there's, so yeah, there are many groups looking at that. They're either, they're different approaches to that, either stimulating the retina in the eye or stimulating the cortex, the visual cortex, or something that my colleague, my good friend, a long-term friend at Mass General, is developing a stimulator that stimulates in between the cortex. and the retina called the thalamus, in the visual thalamus. And that may have certain advantages. So yes, there's a lot going on there.
虽然很接近了。但是有，所以是的，有很多小组在研究它。他们要么，有不同的方法，要么刺激眼睛里的视网膜，要么刺激皮层，视觉皮层，或者我的同事，我的好朋友，在麻省总医院的长期朋友正在开发一种刺激器，刺激皮层和视网膜之间，称为丘脑，在视觉丘脑中。这可能有一些优势。所以是的，那里发生了很多事情。

35
